Environment Dependencies and Compilation
----------------------------------------

This pipeline relies on two core astronomical software stacks: CIAO 
(primarily for spatial data analysis) and HEASOFT (for spectral fitting).

Contbin Compilation Workflow (Must be performed in the CIAO environment):
1. Clone the official 'contbin' source code. 
   You can clone it either parallel to (recommended) or inside the project root directory:
   git clone https://github.com/jeremysanders/contbin.git

2. Navigate to the scripts directory and run the compilation script:
   cd scripts/
   chmod +x build_contbin.sh
   ./build_contbin.sh
   
   *(Note: The script is smart enough to auto-detect the 'contbin' directory 
     in both parallel and internal locations and compile it seamlessly.)*

3. [IMPORTANT] Restart the Conda environment to refresh path hooks:
   conda deactivate && conda activate ciao-fxt

-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=

Pipeline Runtime Environment Requirements
-----------------------------------------

Due to the overlapping environment configurations and potential conflicts 
between CIAO and HEASOFT (e.g., system library paths and alias overrides), 
please strictly follow these requirements when executing scripts:

1. Spatial Binning (pipe1_ciao.py) & Scientific Plotting (pipe3_plot.py):
   Must be run within the CIAO environment.

2. Spectral Extraction & Fitting (pipe2_fxt.py):
   Must be run within the HEASOFT environment.

Please manually switch to the appropriate environment before running each 
script to ensure correct toolchain invocation.


-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=

Data Preparation (data/ directory)
----------------------------------

Before running the pipeline, ensure the data/ directory contains the 
necessary files. Below is a standard directory structure:

data/
├── 11900004574/                           # ObsID 1 data directory
│   ├── bkg_a.pha                          # Background spectrum for Telescope A
│   ├── bkg_b.pha                          # Background spectrum for Telescope B
│   ├── fxt_a_11900004574_...-without_vign.expo  # Exposure map for Telescope A (vignetting-corrected)
│   ├── fxt_a_11900004574_..._cl_3ac.fits  # Event file for Telescope A
│   ├── fxt_b_11900004574_...-without_vign.expo  # Exposure map for Telescope B (vignetting-corrected)
│   └── fxt_b_11900004574_..._cl_3ac.fits  # Event file for Telescope B
├── 11900296704/                           # ObsID 2 data directory
│   ├── bkg_a.pha
│   ├── bkg_b.pha
│   ├── ..._6ca-without_vign.expo
│   ├── ..._cl_6ca.fits
│   ├── ..._6da-without_vign.expo
│   └── ..._cl_6da.fits
├── 11900312064/                           # ObsID 3 data directory and so on
│   └── ...
├── 11904194533/
│   └── ...
├── 11904195707/
│   └── ...
├── 11904195719/
│   └── ...
├── fxt_total_counts.fits                  # Globally merged counts image (used for large FOV spatial binning)
└── pts.reg                                # Global point source exclusion region file (common to all ObsIDs)

The core logic of the automatic observation loading function revolves around dynamically scanning and sorting data
based on your directory structure. It begins by iterating through the contents of the designated data directory, using 
a regular expression to strictly match 11-digit numeric subdirectories, which correspond directly to your distinct Observation
IDs (ObsIDs). For each valid ObsID directory discovered, the script initializes an observation dictionary and maps the 
absolute paths for the fixed background spectra files, namely `bkg_a.pha` and `bkg_b.pha`. It then performs an intelligent
fuzzy sorting mechanism by scanning the rest of the subdirectory's files to accurately pair the respective telescope data 
streams. Files containing the string "*a*" with a `.fits` extension are linked as the Telescope A event file, while those 
containing "*b*" and `.fits` are assigned to Telescope B. In the same manner, exposure maps ending in `.expo` are distinguished 
and mapped into their respective Telescope A and B variables using the "*a*" and "*b*" identifiers. Finally, the function validates
that all six required file paths are successfully located and populated for a given ObsID before appending the dictionary to the
global observation list, ensuring absolute data integrity and completeness before any heavy extraction or fitting tasks can begin.
Auto-Loading Logic
-----------------

  data/{ObsID}/ ─────────────────────────────────────────┐
       │                                                 │
       ├─ bkg_a.pha  ──────────────────────┐             │
       │  (fixed name)                     │ obs_dict    │
       ├─ bkg_b.pha  ──────────────────────┤             │
       │  (fixed name)                     │             │
       ├─ *a*.fits ──────────────────┐    │             │
       │  (evt file, telescope A)     ├────┼─► evt_a    │
       ├─ *b*.fits ──────────────────┤    │             │
       │  (evt file, telescope B)     ├────┼─► evt_b    │
       ├─ *a*.expo ──────────────────┤    │             │
       │  (exposure, telescope A)     ├────┼─► exp_a    │
       └─ *b*.expo ──────────────────┤    │             │
            (exposure, telescope B)  ├────┼─► exp_b    │
                                     │    │             │
                                     └────┴────────────► obs_list[]

  Regex: ^\d{11}$  (11-digit ObsID folder)
  Validation: All 6 keys required before appendings
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=

Configuration (config.json) Reference
-------------------------------------

[base Module]
- center_ra/dec : Target observational coordinates (degrees)
- radius_arcmin : Analysis radius (pixels outside are masked)
- base_image    : Filename of the base counts FITS image (located in data/)

[step1 Module]
- method        : Binning algorithm ("contbin" | "wvt" | "acb")
- region_file   : DS9 region file for point source exclusion
- target_sn     : Target S/N ratio; the system merges pixels until reached
- max_radius    : ACB algorithm constraint; prevents overly large circular bins

[step2 Module]
- downsample    : ACB algorithm step size. Higher values increase speed but 
                  decrease resolution/detail; lower values increase 
                  precision but require more computation time.
- evt_a/b       : Event files for Telescope A/B
- bkg_file_a/b  : Background PHA spectra for Telescope A/B
- exp_file_a/b  : Exposure maps for Telescope A/B

[step4_fit Module]
- nh            : Galactic hydrogen column density (10^22 cm^-2); usually frozen
- kt            : Initial plasma temperature guess (keV)
- abund         : Metal abundance (Solar); usually fixed at 0.3
- redshift      : Cosmological redshift of the target
- norm          : Initial APEC model normalization
- energy_range  : Fitting energy band [min, max] (keV); used to filter low-energy noise